language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ chess_set: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A small joinery makes two different sizes of boxwood chess sets. The small set requires 3 hours of machining on a lathe, and the large set requires 2 hours. There are four lathes with skilled operators who each work a 40 hour week, so there are 160 lathe-hours per week. The small chess set requires 1 kg of boxwood, and the large set requires 3 kg. Only 200 kg of boxwood per week can be obtained. Each large chess set yields a profit of $20, and each small chess set a profit of $5. Decide how many sets of each kind should be made each week so as to maximise profit.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/chess_set.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ the number of lathe-hours available per week
letting lathe_hours = 160
$ the kilograms of boxwood available per week
letting boxwood_kg = 200

$ small_set: the number of small chess sets made per week
find small_set : int(0..200)
$ large_set: the number of large chess sets made per week
find large_set : int(0..200)
$ max_profit: the profit from the sets made per week, which is maximised
find max_profit : int(0..100000)

maximising max_profit
such that
$ the lathe time used by the small and large sets does not exceed the lathe-hours available
3 * small_set + 2 * large_set <= lathe_hours,
$ the boxwood used by the small and large sets does not exceed the boxwood available
small_set + 3 * large_set <= boxwood_kg,
$ the profit is $5 per small set and $20 per large set
max_profit = 5 * small_set + 20 * large_set
